# ── Godot ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Editor-generated import cache and derived data. Godot rebuilds this on open.
.godot/
# Godot 4.4+ drops a .uid beside every script on open. Upstream recommends
# committing them, but this repo's update flow (tools/update_mac.sh) already
# promises they stay untracked local files — one convention, kept.
*.uid

# Legacy Godot 3 import folder, harmless to keep ignored
.import/

# Exported builds — never commit these
build/
builds/
export/
exports/
*.exe
*.pck
*.zip
*.apk
*.aab
*.dmg
*.app

# Godot export credentials — contains keystore paths and passwords
export_presets.cfg

# Android/iOS build templates
/android/
/ios/

# ── OS / editors ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# ── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.local

# ── Notes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Art, audio, and video are tracked via Git LFS — see .gitattributes.
# Do NOT commit large binaries directly. A previous project in this repo hit
# 556MB of history that way, and the only fix was throwing the history out.

# ── Prototype art: TEMPORARILY UNTRACKED ─────────────────────────────────────
# The 333 package assets are NOT committed yet. This remote build environment's
# network policy blocks lfs.github.com, so LFS objects cannot be uploaded from
# here — and committing ~51MB of PNGs as raw blobs would defeat .gitattributes
# and need a history rewrite to undo.
#
# Re-confirmed: the batch API answers through the local git proxy, but the
# object upload itself still redirects to lfs.github.com and returns Forbidden.
#
# TO COMMIT THEM (from your own machine, where LFS works):
#   1. Delete this block from .gitignore
#   2. ./tools/restore_package_assets.sh <first-playtest-package-or-zip>
#   3. ./tools/install_sheet_package.py <all-11-sheets-package-or-zip>
#   4. git lfs install
#   5. git add assets && git commit -m "chore: add prototype assets via LFS"
#   6. git push
#
# .gitattributes already routes these through LFS, so step 5 stores pointers.
# Step 3 needs Pillow and numpy; it keys the chroma background those sheets
# ship with, so copying them by hand instead will produce green frames.
#
# The four generated effect sheets in assets/vfx/realtime/ are caught by the same
# rule, and they are the one thing here that cannot be restored from a package you
# already have. Rebuild them with:
#
#   # download the four clips listed in docs/VFX_SOURCES.json -> some directory
#   ./tools/vfx_from_video.py --sources <that directory>
#   godot --headless --path . --import
#   godot --headless --path . --script scripts/tools/build_additive_vfx.gd
#
# docs/VFX_SOURCES.json carries the source URL, the job id and every build
# decision, so the sheets come back byte-for-byte rather than approximately.
assets/

# Python bytecode from the import tooling
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Local Python virtualenv, if one is made in-project
.venv/

# MCP server config is COMMITTED, so a fresh clone has the tools wired up.
#
# It was ignored when it held machine-specific paths to a local Godot MCP binary.
# It now holds only URLs and ${VAR} references, which Claude Code expands from the
# environment at startup — so the repo carries the wiring and the shell carries the
# secrets. See docs/PIXELLAB_SETUP.md.
#
# The rule that makes this safe: a value in .mcp.json is NEVER a literal secret.
# If a key ever needs to go in, it goes in as ${SOMETHING_API_KEY} and lives in
# ~/.zshrc. Anything else belongs in .mcp.local.json, which stays ignored.
.mcp.local.json
